A||||8||TRiAD||Harnessbelts...||||5||
Z||000000||TRiAD||06-03-2001||08:25 PM||||Schroth harnessbelts are one of the best investments I've ever made on a project. <BR>Luckilly I've never needed to "test" them out, but they are DOT legal in all 50 states, as long as the OE belts are still installed. (You can wear the Schroth's by themselves, but the other belts must be installed). <P>Anyway, they are designed to be mounted using the lap-belt connection for the rear seats, and this will be the first 2-seater I've put them into. <BR>Question, does anyone have these in theirs, and how did you mount them?<P>Thanks!<P>------------------<BR><B><I>Michael ~ triadtuning@hotmail.com</I></B><BR>-----------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88FieroGTwhitefscustomcroppedmini.jpg"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||TRiAD||06-04-2001||10:29 PM||||Anyone?<P>Thanks!||||reg||5
Z||000002||cadaver||06-05-2001||10:49 PM||||Nope I have Deist harness's. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||PJ||06-05-2001||11:55 PM||||In a car with rear seats the front shoulder belts attach to the rear lap belt anchor points or the rear belts themselves, I've seen both. I don't think there is a safe way to mount dual shoulder belts in a Fiero without a roll bar behind the seats. The only mounting points available are thin sheet metal.<P>PJ<BR>ITA #66 Black<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||TRiAD||06-06-2001||06:14 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by PJ:<BR><B>In a car with rear seats the front shoulder belts attach to the rear lap belt anchor points or the rear belts themselves, I've seen both. I don't think there is a safe way to mount dual shoulder belts in a Fiero without a roll bar behind the seats. The only mounting points available are thin sheet metal.<P>PJ<BR>ITA #66 Black</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That's what I was thinking...do you know of a rollbar for these cars? (was planning on nit anyway)<BR>First choice would be Autopower, if they make one...I'll look too. <BR>Thanks!<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||PJ||06-06-2001||10:47 AM||||I don't think Autopower sells one. I got mine from Al Kirk in Birmingham, AL a long time ago. I believe his latest design has some clearance issues with the ECM. Call him up and ask him. 205/608-1156.<P>PJ<BR>ITA #66 Black<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||1FST2M6||06-06-2001||01:06 PM||||  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/corbeau.jpg">  <P>i had MOMO harnesses with my Corbeau seats.. they were SFI approved. and harness SUCK for a daily driver... you can't get your wallet if you leave it in your pocket to pay for your taco.. your keysare a pain to get to if you leave them in your pocket.. and don't try to reach the ATM. <P>but they STOMP NUTZ at the track.. deep in a corner and your still square in the seat..   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 06-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||1FST2M6||06-06-2001||01:13 PM|||| oh yeah.. <A HREF="http://www.ompwins.com" TARGET=_blank>www.ompwins.com</A>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <BR> <A HREF="http://www.kirkracing.com" TARGET=_blank>www.kirkracing.com</A>  they also have a roll cage for our cars.. I've install quite a few in the NIRA and SCCA (HSR SVRA Cup Lite and IT) cars we have at the shop.. they do GREAT work.<p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 06-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||TRiAD||06-06-2001||04:18 PM||||Thanks guys!<BR>I know all about the drawbacks,  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I have a permenantly broken sternum, and must wear harnesses basically at all times, if I want to be really OK. It was broken by a regular belt, so taking another hit form a regular belt could be...bad. <P>I appreciate the info! Especially on the bar, I'll call him. ||||reg||1
